<title>ROMANIA: Romania to use bourse to sell 17 companies.</title>
<headline>Romania to use bourse to sell 17 companies.</headline>
<dateline>BUCHAREST 1997-07-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Romania's main privatisation agency said on Tuesday it would use the Bucharest bourse to sell 17 firms in various sectors, including machine building, wood processing, drilling and textiles.</p>
<p>In a statement, the State Owneship Fund (FPS) said it would offer starting September a first batch of four companies, which it did not identify, from the 17 earmarked for the sell-off.</p>
<p>The move is part of a broader FPS project to sell around 1,000 companies through the local capital markets by end-1997.</p>
<p>The FPS has a controlling stake in more than 6,500 state firms up for eventual privatisation.</p>
<p>Following is the list of the 17 companies:</p>
<p>	Company		Location/county	 Sector</p>
<p>    1. Altur		    Olt	     Machine building</p>
<p>    2. Aparataj electric    Dambovita     Electrical equipment</p>
<p>    3. Automecanica	   Sibiu	   Metalic constructions</p>
<p>    4. Cildro		   Mehedinti     Wood processing</p>
<p>    5. Ciprom		   Prahova	 Drilling equipment</p>
<p>    6. Cominco		  Bucharest     Building</p>
<p>    7. FEPA		     Vaslui	  Machine building</p>
<p>    8. Grimex		   Gorj	    Mining equipment</p>
<p>    9. Hitrom		   Vaslui	  Insulation materials</p>
<p>   10. ICME		     Bucharest     Electrical equipment</p>
<p>   11. Mobstrat		 Suceava	 Wood processing</p>
<p>   12. Novatex		  Arges	   Textiles</p>
<p>   13. Robinete Industriale Bacau	   Machine building</p>
<p>   14. Romceram		 Bucharest     Building materials</p>
<p>   15. Romfor		   Prahova	 Drilling</p>
<p>   16. SCT			Bucharest     Telecoms repairs</p>
<p>   17. TMC			Dolj	    Electrical equipment</p>
<p>-- Bucharest newsroom 40-1 3120264</p>
